      <description>&lt;h2 id=&#34;how-to-use-interval-in-postgresql&#34;&gt;How To Use INTERVAL in PostgreSQL&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The INTERVAL data type in PostgreSQL allows you to store and manipulate periods of time. In this article, we&amp;rsquo;ll explore how to use INTERVAL in PostgreSQL for various time-related operations.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h3 id=&#34;practical-example&#34;&gt;Practical Example&lt;/h3&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-sql&#34; data-lang=&#34;sql&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;-- Example: Calculating the difference between two timestamps&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;TIMESTAMP&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;&amp;#39;2024-01-21 12:00:00&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;TIMESTAMP&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;&amp;#39;2024-01-20 10:30:00&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;AS&lt;/span&gt; time_difference;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;In this example, we subtract one timestamp from another to get the time difference.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h3 id=&#34;faqs-on-using-interval-in-postgresql&#34;&gt;FAQs on Using INTERVAL in PostgreSQL&lt;/h3&gt;&#xA;&lt;div itemscope itemtype=&#34;https://schema.org/FAQPage&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;    &lt;div itemscope itemtype=&#34;https://schema.org/Question&#34; itemprop=&#34;mainEntity&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;      &lt;h2 itemprop=&#34;name&#34;&gt;Q: How can 